# Slice 334 (the author 2026-11-09: `name: "test"` with an existing callable name obeys the ordinary call of that
# callable): with the function name declared, the part `name: name(7)` holds the call name(7), applied once where the
# traversal reaches the receiver's Frame; its value binds no part name, and the receiver is refused (the interpreter
# answers 5; native and forced code fail there). Since M6 (slice 344: only f(args) calls) the call is written name(7):
# the colon Frame `name: 7` of slice 334 calls nothing.
fn: name (int: x) int (int: calls 0)
    calls: calls + 1
    return: x
end: name
int: r1 1
fn: name: name(7); args: (int: a); return: int; body: (return: a)
int: r2 2
